==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|ic|ih|ich||Northumbrian}}
* {{alter|ang|iċċ}}
* {{alt|ang|ᛁᚳ||{{w|Ruthwell Cross}}}}, {{alter|ang|ᛁᚴ}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*ik}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*ik}}, unstressed form of {{m|gem-pro|*ek}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*éǵh₂}}. 

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|iċ}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|ang|pronoun|cat2=personal pronouns|head=iċ}}

# [[I]]
#: {{ux|ang|'''Iċ''' lufiġe þē.|'''I''' love you.}}
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n203/mode/2up John 6:20]
#*: {{quote|ang|'''Iċ''' hit eom. Ne ondrǣdaþ ēow.|It's '''me''' [literally '''''I''' am it'']. Don't be scared.}}
#* ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=i_FBtx0ZPPMC&pg=PA12 The Life of Saint Margaret]''
#*: {{quote|ang|'''Iċ''' nylle nān word mā of þīnum mūðe ġehīeran.|'''I''' don't want to hear one more word out of your mouth.}}

====Usage notes====
* In modern English, object pronouns are often used as subjects in a wide variety of circumstances ("<u>Me</u> and <u>her</u> are friends", "you're as big as <u>me</u>"). In Old English, only subject pronouns were used as subjects (except with a small class of verbs such as {{m|ang|līcian}}, {{m|ang|mǣtan}}, and {{m|ang|twēoġan}}, which took dative or accusative subjects with nouns and pronouns alike). Thus "me and her are friends" was ''<u>Iċ</u> and <u>hēo</u> sind ġefrīend'', literally "<u>I</u> and <u>she</u> are friends."

